WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
In the last episode,

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:04.560
we learned about TFX, which
helps us engineer our machine

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.210
learning pipeline
so we can deploy

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.140
our models in production.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.550
Are you ready to see
it all in action?

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:11.517
Let's go.

00:00:11.517 --> 00:00:14.439
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.340
Welcome to A.I.
Adventures, where

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.500
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.620
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and today I'm

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.590
going to show you how to manage
a production machine learning

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.270
pipeline using TFX.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.730
For this, we are going
to use the Chicago Taxi

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.920
Dataset, available in BitQuery.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.010
And the goal of our model
is to predict if customers

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.390
tip more or less than 20%.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.560
So it is a binary
classification problem.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.030
Here's what we will do.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.390
First, we will set up our
environment on Google Cloud.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.260
Then we'll set up and deploy
A.I. pipelines on a Kubernetes

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.240
cluster using Kubeflow.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.480
Then, we set up Cloud A.I.
Platform, Notebook Instance,

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.770
and work in the notebook
to run the pipeline.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.840
We hop into the
Google Cloud console

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.810
and create an A.I.
platform pipeline cluster.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.060
Configure the Kubeflow pipeline.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.120
Make sure to allow
access to the Cloud APIs.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.190
This is an important
step for the cluster

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.930
to access other
pieces of our project.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.560
Once the cluster is created,
we deploy the pipeline.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.050
Now we open our pipeline
dashboard and the TF notebook.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:30.110
We create a new
notebook instance here.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.680
You can change the machine type
here to a faster processing

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.650
machine, but remember that
there's a cost associated

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.090
with your choice.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:40.460
Now we are in our notebook.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.140
The first step is to install TFX
and Kubeflow pipeline packages.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.570
Downloading Scaffold
module, we then

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:51.350
set the path to include user
Python binary directory,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.720
and a directory
containing Scaffold.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.710
Then we check which version
of TFX is installed.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:01.430
And then set the project ID
as the environment variable.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.560
Set the Kubeflow
cluster endpoint,

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.380
and then we set the name
for the custom Docker

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.910
image of our pipeline.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:11.000
And now we set the
name of our pipeline

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.750
and copy the pipeline template
into the project directory.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.560
Today, for this example, we're
using Chicago Taxi Dataset

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.820
to perform binary
classification.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.690
So the template sets
the model to taxi.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.580
As a result of this command,
in the file browser,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.120
we see a basic
scaffold of files that

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.200
build a pipeline, including
pipeline source codes, sample

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.330
data, and Jupyter notebooks.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.370
Now, if you're working
on a different problem,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.040
these files might look
a little bit different.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.890
Here, let's see what
these files are.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.520
Pipeline directory contains
the definition of the pipeline.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.200
configs.py the common constants
for the pipeline runners.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.440
pipeline.py defines
the TFX components,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.160
and a pipeline models
directory contains the machine

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.050
learning model definitions.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:05.090
features.py and features_test.py
defines the met features

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.530
of the model.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:10.850
preprocessing.py and
preprocessing_test.py defines

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.270
feature engineering
using TFX transform.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:17.750
Estimated directory contains
an estimator-based model.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.980
Inside that, constants.py
defines constants of the model,

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.900
and model.py and model_test.py
defines the DNN model using

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.820
the TensorFlow estimator.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.710
Keras directory contains
a Keras-based model,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.120
which is constants.py
file, defines the constants

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.170
of the model, and the model.py
and model_test.py defines

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.630
the DNN model using Keras.

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:45.860
Kubeflow_dag_runner file defines
runners for each orchestration

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:46.670
engine.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.910
This example includes both
an estimator-based model

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.010
and a Keras-based
model for reference.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.740
So you should choose
whichever one is best for you.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.850
Now, before we run our
TFX pipeline on Kubeflow,

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.790
we first need somewhere to
store the artifacts that

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.160
will be created when
we run the pipeline.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.960
These artifacts are
called payloads,

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.150
and we will use Google
Cloud Storage to store them.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.900
We copy our sample data
to GCS, and then we

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.650
create the pipeline.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.480
This will generate a
Docker file and build.yaml

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.940
to build a Docker image.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:23.420
Make sure to add these files
to your source code system,

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.840
for example, Git, along
with other source files.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.170
Now we are ready to run
our TFX pipeline using

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.730
the TFX run create command.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.050
We can also see this run under
experiments in the Kubeflow

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.380
pipelines dashboard.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.860
Now, the first task in any data
science or machine learning

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:44.800
project is to understand
and clean the data.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.550
ExampleGen ingests and
splits the input data set.

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.870
StatisticsGen calculates
statistics for the data set.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.090
SchemaGen examines
the statistics

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.490
and creates a data schema.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.510
Example Validator looks for the
anomalies and missing values

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.280
inside the data set.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.300
In the pipeline.py file, we
will enable these components

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.450
and update the
pipeline for a rerun.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:15.340
After the rerun, we can see our
pipeline in the visual format.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.110
And after understanding
the data and cleaning it,

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:22.660
the next step is to transform to
increase the predictive quality

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:26.020
of our data, and reduce the
dimensionality and feature

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.220
engineering.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:30.580
After transforming the data,
we use the Trainer component

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.290
of the TFX to train our model.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.180
Then we would run Evaluator
to perform deep analysis

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:38.350
of the training results.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:41.740
And then we use the Pusher
component to deploy the model

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.540
to a serving infrastructure.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.600
I enabled all these
in the pipeline.py,

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.330
and the resulting pipeline,
with all the components,

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.820
looks like this in the
Kubeflow dashboard.

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:54.010
Depending on the requirements
of your workload,

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:58.210
your pipeline might need
more CPU, GPUs, or memory.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:01.960
To modify these, I have included
the documentation below.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.990
Now that we know how
the TFX pipeline works,

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.720
you might be wondering
how we can directly use

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.060
BigQuery as the data source.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.550
There's a component
called BigQuery Example

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.450
Gen that connects to BigQuery
in a specified Google Cloud

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:17.920
project.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.290
TFX inter-operates
with several other

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:23.800
managed Google Cloud Services,
such as Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:26.470
Several TFX components
use Apache Beam

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.990
to implement data
parallel pipelines,

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.690
and it means that you can
distribute data processing

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.600
workloads using Cloud Dataflow.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.930
TFX also interoperates
with Cloud A.I. Platform

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.790
for training and prediction.

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.410
You can set your
Trainer component

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:44.170
to use Cloud A.I.
Platform training, which

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.450
is a managed service for
training machine learning

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.230
models.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:50.750
And when your model is built
and ready to be served,

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.900
you can push your model to
Cloud A.I. Platform Prediction

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.120
for serving.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.730
In this video, we
just created, and ran,

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:00.160
a machine learning
pipeline, and visualized

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:01.810
the results of each run.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.360
Give TFX a try yourself
with a different data

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.515
set, and let me know how it
goes in the comments below.

00:07:07.515 --> 00:07:08.890
If you like this
video, and would

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:13.200
like to see more such content,
give us a like and subscribe.

